item.content:encoded:
●  Clean core extensibility is when we, as a design choice, decide to go that way per use case: synchronous OData for transactional use cases that require immediate response, asynchronous events for all other use cases.●   At the beginning of each CAPM project, multi-tenancy issues should be addressed; retrofitting tenant isolation in a single-tenant already deployed extension is a much more disruptive affair than to include the feature from the word go.●   Principal propagation does not feature in the design of which extensions preserve core system authorization and audit trails’ integrity versus which ones allow them to degrade silently. ●  In CAPM extensions the great production risk is not in the framework at all but in the peripheral issues: destination config, XSUAA scope mapping, CDS to core field mapping which go out of sync across environments.●  Clean core, which reduces upgrade risk in the ERP core but does not remove operational burden; it shifts versioning, monitoring, and authorization complexity into the BTP environment, which in turn requires the same operational care as the core itself.SAP’s clean-core strategy tells engineering teams to leave S/4HANA itself largely untouched. Custom logic should run beside it as applications on SAP Business Technology Platform, usually built with the Cloud Application Programming Model (CAPM). That change is not merely structural. Teams now have to deal with distributed-system problems—network requests instead of local function calls, tenant isolation, token-based authorization, and eventual consistency—in a domain long shaped by monolithic customization. This article examines recurring design choices in production CAPM extension projects, including how extensions compose services with the core, how tenants are separated, how SAP Event Mesh carries integration events, and how authorization relies on principal propagation. The discussion comes from hands-on experience designing and building an extension from start to finish, with architects and senior engineers in mind—people who need to make these tradeoffs correctly on the first attempt.For most of the past two decades, extending SAP meant changing the ERP system itself. Teams added ABAP reports, user exits, and Z-tables alongside the vendor’s standard code. The approach worked, but the trade-off was familiar: each custom object could fail during the next upgrade, and the line separating SAP’s code from the company’s own gradually disappeared.Keep the ERP core to stock standards, and put custom logic into a separate cloud native runtime, SAP BTP, which can be stood up or taken down independent of the core’s release cycle [1]. CAPM is what SAP uses for that outer layer: a domain model-first, convention over configuration approach which compiles Core Data Services (CDS) models into OData services, with persistence, authorization and multitenancy mainly handled by generated code [2].The problem is simple: “move the logic to BTP” is a slogan, not an architecture. Accepting it leaves a team to settle the details that improvised projects often mishandle: connecting the extension to the core, serving several business units from one codebase without exposing their data to one another, carrying a user’s identity across the network, and monitoring a service beyond the familiar SAP Basis toolchain. Without a deliberate design, teams tend to rely on synchronous calls, technical users rather than propagated authorization, and single-tenant deployments. The second consumer then arrives, and the design has to be rebuilt. These are the same distributed-systems errors organizations make during a first microservices project—only they reach the SAP ecosystem later.Service design, multi-tenancy, event based integration, and authorization of principals we also look at what goes wrong in production. This article looks at the most important issues: Standard configuration and key-user (in-app) extensibility is what we have in S/4HANA; for anything that requires custom code or a new data model we go to side by side extensions on BTP [1], [5]. The RESTful ABAP Programming Model (RAP) is the clean core compatible way to expose logic that must stay in ABAP, which we build on CDS views and which generates OData services with transactional (draft, lock, validation) handling [5], [9].In the world of side-by-side half we have CAPM which is the model-driven framework. A CAPM project puts forth entities and services in CDS; the framework in turn puts out an OData v4 (or v2) service with default persistence and generic CRUD handlers which also has in it hooks for custom logic in Node.js or Java [2], [4]. Also it is typical for CAPM apps to run on Cloud Foundry or Kyma, which in turn is backed by SAP HANA Cloud, and we also see Fiori elements UIs generated from the same CDS annotations.SAP Event Mesh is the managed version of the event-broker pattern which we have in BTP middleware that puts together event delivery between producers and subscribers so their availability and load are put out of each other’s business. It is the typical means for asynchronous integration between the core (which is publishing business events) and extensions that may react without polling.XSUAA, the SAP Authorization and Trust Management service, is BTP’s OAuth2 auth server: it issues and issues JWTs which have in them scopes from role collections and is what CAPM apps use for auth and auth requests [6], [7].Four problems recur across CAPM extension projects, independent of industry:Where does the transaction boundary sit? A parallel extension is not in the same unit of work as the S/4HANA tables it depends on. What used to be a single local transaction now requires a synchronous call with its own error handling, or an asynchronous flow which includes eventual consistency. Teams that ignore this design step implement synchronous calls into every operation which in turn caps the extension’s availability to that of the core system.How many tenants does the extension actually serve? In many cases, a single business unit’s extension is also required by a second unit or country within the first year. If tenant isolation was not a primary design issue from the start, then as we go in to retrofit it we are looking at re-provisioning infrastructure and re-testing authorization, which at this point the first tenant is already in production. Whose identity is on the wire? Calling S/4HANA through a broad-privilege technical user is easier than setting up identity federation, which explains its popularity. But it creates a serious authorization flaw: everyone shares one credential, so individual checks disappear and the audit trail points to the technical account rather than the person who made the change.Who is watching the extension once it is live? A CAP service does not inherit the monitoring practices that come with the ABAP stack; a Basis team’s muscle memory for ST22 reports does not translate to Cloud Foundry logs, which in turn must be deliberately wired into an observability pipeline. In the case of S/4HANA, we maintain minimal custom logic (standard objects, ABAP CDS views, RAP exposed business objects), which in turn has a side-by-side CAPM extension in a BTP subaccount for UI, extended logic, and any data model that does not fit in the ERP schema.Three decisions define this shape. Service composition: The extension connects to the core in two ways. Requests that need an immediate response use OData v2/v4 through the Destination and Connectivity services. Notifications go through SAP Event Mesh instead. In practice, asynchronous communication should be the default; use synchronous calls only when the caller cannot continue without the result, because they tie the extension’s SLA to a system outside its control.Multitenancy: CAPM's @sap/cds-mtxs package implements SAP's standard pattern — one provider subaccount running the extension, an MTX sidecar handling tenant onboarding/upgrade, and one isolated database container per tenant subaccount [3], [10], [12]. This we see a shared application, isolated data model which is more economical than one deployment per tenant and we keep tenant data separate which is a point of note for the compliance reviewers.Authorization boundary: XSUAA-issued JWTs authorize the extension's own endpoints; the Destination service, configured for principal propagation, carries the caller's identity through to the core so OData calls execute — and are authorization-checked and logged — as the actual end user [6], [7], [8].The tradeoff: this trades in-core simplicity for operational surface area — a second deployable, security model, and monitoring domain, in exchange for upgrade independence. That trade is worth making for most net-new logic; it is not automatically worth making for a small, stable customization cheaper to maintain in place, a point returned to below.The following snippets illustrate the patterns above at the level of real project code.Domain model keyed against a core identifier, not a replica of core data:// db/schema.cds
 namespace com.example.extension;
 using { cuid, managed } from '@sap/cds/common';
  
 entity RiskAssessments : cuid, managed {
   salesOrder   : String(10) @title: 'S/4HANA Sales Order';
   riskScore	: Decimal(3,0);
   reviewStatus : String(20) default 'OPEN';
   reviewedBy   : String(256);
   notes    	: String(1000);
 }
The entity carries a plain salesOrder string rather than an association into a replicated copy of the order table — replicating core master data reintroduces exactly the coupling clean core is meant to remove. The extension resolves the current state on demand instead.Synchronous read-through to the core via a remote OData service:// srv/external/S4SalesOrder.cds
 using { API_SALES_ORDER_SRV as external } from './S4SalesOrder.csn';
 service S4Service {
   entity SalesOrders as projection on external.A_SalesOrder;
 }
// srv/risk-service.js
 const cds = require('@sap/cds');
 module.exports = cds.service.impl(async function () {
   const { RiskAssessments } = this.entities;
   this.on('READ', RiskAssessments, async (req, next) => {
 	const results = await next();
 	const s4 = await cds.connect.to('S4Service');
 	for (const row of results) {
   	const order = await s4.run(
         SELECT.one.from('SalesOrders').where({ SalesOrder: row.salesOrder })
   	);
   	row.orderStatus = order?.OverallSDProcessStatus;
 	}
 	return results;
   });
 });
cds.connect.to('S4Service') resolves, via configuration, to a destination that carries the inbound user's principal — propagation is configuration, not application code, so the same handler runs correctly whether the destination uses principal propagation or, in a lower environment, a technical user.Reacting to a core event asynchronously, instead of polling:// srv/event-listener.js
 const cds = require('@sap/cds');
 module.exports = cds.service.impl(async function () {
   const messaging = await cds.connect.to('messaging');
   messaging.on('sap.s4.beh.salesorder.v1.SalesOrder.Changed.v1', async (msg) => {
 	const { SalesOrder } = msg.data;
 	await UPDATE(cds.entities.RiskAssessments)
   	.set({ reviewStatus: 'NEEDS_REVIEW' })
   	.where({ salesOrder: SalesOrder });
   });
 });
This handler updates the extension's own state and lets a downstream process act on it — decoupling that keeps the extension available even during a core maintenance window.Authorization declared next to the model it protects:// srv/risk-service.cds
 service RiskService @(requires: 'authenticated-user') {
   entity RiskAssessments as projection on extension.RiskAssessments;
   annotate RiskAssessments with @(
 	restrict: [
   	{ grant: ['READ'], to: 'RiskViewer' },
   	{ grant: ['*'],	to: 'RiskManager' }
 	]
   );
 }
RiskViewer/RiskManager map, via xs-security.json, to XSUAA scopes and role collections assigned to real users:{
   "scopes": [
 	{ "name": "$XSAPPNAME.RiskViewer", "description": "Read risk assessments" },
 	{ "name": "$XSAPPNAME.RiskManager", "description": "Manage risk assessments" }
   ],
   "role-templates": [
 	{ "name": "RiskViewer", "scope-references": ["$XSAPPNAME.RiskViewer"] },
 	{ "name": "RiskManager", "scope-references": ["$XSAPPNAME.RiskManager"] }
   ]
 }
These role templates are what an administrator turns into actual role collections and assigns to users or groups in the BTP cockpit — CDS declares intent, this file is the contract USAA enforces against, and the role collection assignment is the step most likely to drift out of sync with the other two, as the Production Lessons Learned section below discusses.CAPM applications scale the way most stateless Cloud Foundry/Kyma services do: horizontally, with session state kept out of the application tier. The design questions that matter are elsewhere. Read-through calls to the core are the dominant latency cost, not the extension's own logic — a short-lived cache for infrequently changing master data reduces both, with a TTL reflecting how stale the UI may be for that field. One container per tenant which reduces cross-tenant contention but at the same time does require capacity planning against the largest tenant’s needs. Event consumption throughput is a function of the consumer which in turn means that for bursty events there must be sufficient number of consumer instances to handle the volume within the business’s freshness requirement. For bulk operations use OData’s $batch instead of individual calls in a loop which in turn results in one round trip as opposed to hundreds.Security in this architecture is a matter of the full request path maintaining the same guarantees which in turn each individual element of that path is responsible for and not of any single control. ●  Token validation is automatic; scope design is not. At each point of entry we require a valid XSUAA JWT, also it is CAPM’s job to check signature, expiry, and audience which we do without custom code but still we have to write out xs-security.json scope definitions carefully because a very wide default scope renders CDS @restrict annotations useless [6].● Principal propagation should be the default backend authentication mode, Not an issue that is addressed only as an after thought [7], [8]; any technical or user fallback should be put in place as a documented reviewed exception, not a silent default.●  Authorization granularity matters more than authentication. OWASP’s API Security Top 10 puts out broken object level authorization and broken function level authorization as its top priority issues [14] which in turn map directly to CDS @restrict annotations that are too broad, or which are absent at all on a generated navigation property.●  Secrets live in the platform, not the codebase. Destination credentials and API keys go into BTP’s credential store and injected service bindings which also means they should not be in the CDS model or a checked in config file. ● Transport security needs attention at both ends. BTP by default enforces TLS 1.2+ on inbound traffic; for outbound connection via the Cloud Connector that is the team’s responsibility as far as certificate rotation goes, not the platform’s.●  Zero-trust framing fits this architecture naturally. NIST SP 800-207 puts forth zero trust access in terms of per session and identity which is a different approach from that of network location [19] we present this model which by design has the application logging and the core no longer on the same trusted network segment.A CAP service is only as operable as the observability wired around it, and BTP does not wire that in for free.●  Structured, correlated logging. The Application Logging feature provides for search of logs across instances, but only if correlation IDs travel with each request through every downstream call which includes outbound OData calls to the core [24] without that a single reported issue from a user may not be traced across the full request path.●  Distributed tracing matters more here than in a typical microservices system, because a meaningful share of "slow" requests are slow in the core, not the extension, and only a trace spanning both sides of the boundary proves which one [21].●  Alerting should be scoped to actionable symptoms — We see that for elevated error rate, queue backlog, destination authentication failures not all anomalies the platform is technically able to emit; alert fatigue, which leads to practice defeat much faster than any one missed incident.●   Multitenant upgrade rollout needs a staged path. MTX-powered schema upgrades go out to all enrolled tenants at the same time by default, which is why we require a canary tenant (or a small set) for an upgrade to go through before it is made available to all.●  HA/DR is the team's own responsibility. For critical business extensions, we need to see a defined RTO/RPO and a proven out-of-band recovery method; living on a cloud platform does not, in and of itself, guarantee resilience.Trade-offs and Limitations     Clean core, side-by-side extension architecture is what we should go with for new custom logic implementation, but that is not a hard and fast rule. It introduces a network hit and an availability issue which core customizations did not have; in a use case which requires the same transactional consistency as native ABAP logic, forcing it side by side actually trades off upgrade risk for distributed transaction complexity which may not be a clear win. Also, it is not a free ride; each extension is a second thing to secure, patch, and staff. Multi-tenancy issues are a waste of effort for a tool which is very scoped and truly single-tenant, and event-driven integration trades off speed for resilience, which is the wrong trade-off when the business is looking for a sync answer. Small, stable, rarely used custom codes are often better left as minimal in core extensions, then re-platformed for strategic conformity -- clean core is a direction to move logic towards over time, not a reason to do a big bang migration of everything at once.Production Lessons LearnedIn many cases we saw two failure patterns which reported back often enough in real CAPM extension builds to be worth going into in detail; we thought at first they were application bugs in either of these and in fact they were not.The "it works in every environment except the one that matters" destination bug. A CAP service may read from S/4HANA without trouble in development and quality assurance, then fail silently—or return a misleading error—when it reaches production. The application code is often fine. More commonly, production has a destination with the wrong authentication setup: basic authentication was configured instead of principal propagation, or a certificate that works in QA was never replaced with the production certificate. Development and QA tests still pass because each one uses its own correctly configured destination. The problem appears only in the environment that no one tests again before go-live. A lasting fix means managing destination configuration like versioned application code. Each change needs review, and every promotion should include a checklist confirming the authentication method and certificate validity for each landscape—not a cockpit setting configured once and then forgotten.The silent authorization drift between the CDS model and the role collections that were actually assigned. A CDS @restrict is a statement of intent which the actual behavior a user sees is based on the role collection which an admin put together in the identity provider and which two which are edited in different tools by different people at different times. Early in an extension's life the mapping between CDS scopes and role collections is usually correct as one team designed both at the same time. Months down the line after a business request for a new field or operation which a developer then adds to the @restrict annotation which he extends the CDS scope for -- the corresponding role collection update is easy to miss what breaks is not immediate but the new feature just does not show up for anyone which is reported as a "bug in the new feature" instead of being identified as an authorization gap. The best fix is a single mapping document which is owned by someone and reviewed with the security team on a set cadence not just at go live so that a CDS change and its corresponding role collection change are always done together instead of independently.An issue we see is that what began as a solution for one team grew to require a second and by that point the migration was much more difficult we had wished we had put tenant awareness in from the beginning. Also we note that Fiori elements really do speed up UI delivery when the backend CDS @UI annotations are used as designed teams which ignore this and go to manual UI coding see no benefit at all out of the framework which they may have thought would be the solution for them.●  Default to async, event based integration for notifications; use sync OData for interactions that require an immediate response. ●  Multitenancy model to be determined before writing the first line of extension code, which in turn may be for a single tenant launch.●  Principal propagation to be the default back-end auth mode out of which technical user fall back will be a documented exception.●   Write out CDS @restrict annotations for what the security team has approved in terms of role collection design, and at a set interval go over that mapping. ●  For destination and connectivity config, version and review the same care that we put into application code. ●   Put in place distributed tracing that spans the extension-to-core boundary from the very first production deployment, not only after the first latency issue.●    Cache core master data reads with an explicit, business-justified TTL.●   Treat HA/DR posture as an explicit requirement with a stated RTO/RPO, sized to the process's business criticality.SAP reports that it is presenting RAP (which is for logic that must stay in ABAP Cloud) and CAPM (which is for logic that belongs side by side) as embodiments of the same clean-core philosophy which we see in the use of CDS as the model language. As cross-system communication trends towards event-based models, extensions which are put together in a synchronous-first approach will see growth in the need to include asynchronous elements. Also, these trends do not change the base principles on which we operate; they, in fact, put into higher value the issues of service composition and multitenancy from the start.In a side-by-side layer, a team owns end-to-end, not scattered through a vendor-maintained core. CAPM makes that layer a material improvement over hand-rolled middleware, but it does not do away with the underlying distributed systems issues: service composition, tenant isolation, identity propagation, and observability still have to be designed into the layer. Teams which approach those design issues with the same care as any other production distributed system do in fact end up with extensions that are truly upgrade independent; teams which see CAPM as a drag-and-drop solution tend to learn the hard way that these issues did not go away.The author is a senior level SAP engineering consultant which has for 20 plus years’ experience in enterprise SAP development across the US, Singapore, Hong Kong, and the Middle East, we also find that he is a specialist in SAP Fiori, SAPUI5, OData, ABAP, ABAP CDS, the RESTful ABAP Programming Model and cloud native extension architecture on SAP Business Technology Platform which he works with via the Cloud Application Programming Model. Also he has SAP Certified Development Associate credentials in SAP Fiori Application Development and SAP Cloud Platform, an SAP Certified Technology Associate in SAP Fiori System Administration, an SAP Certified Application Associate in SAP Analytics Cloud and is a Certified SAFe 5 Advanced Scrum Master. Also included in his work is the design and development of side by side BTP extensions as part of clean core initiatives on large scale enterprise projects which also included service composition, OData based integration with S/4HANA, and authorization and role design.

Our courier app launched on the iPhone Duo simulator but there was no back button on the Messages screen. The details sheet covered the navigation controls. On a map, part of the route could easily be out of view.These were gaps in our app. We wanted to fix them before marking the app ready for the Duo. Much of the work involved how our existing interface worked in a differently-shaped window. Once the map, sheets and navigation started to play well together, we could produce a screenshot of the app for the App Store in an unfolded state.In our driver screens we show a route on a map, delivery details, messages and actions for proof of delivery. On a narrow iPhone, a bottom sheet lets the driver switch between the details and the map. On the Duo, both could be shown at once.Opening the app was the beginningIn this wider view, the relationship between the views changed. At times, the system would present the sheet next to the map. And navigation buttons could appear in a space occupied by the sheet. In the narrow view, we had assumptions about this layout. They no longer held up.We started this work using the iOS 27.1 Duo simulator and Xcode 27.1 beta tooling. The observations below are of that tooling, not necessarily of every release of Xcode or of every piece of Duo hardware.We didn't save the version of the beta build used to take our screenshots. You could check Apple's release notes for Xcode 27.1 and test for tooling issues in that version.Work out where the sheet actually isIn our first pass at this, we used size classes to work out whether the sheet would be docked. In three places in our code we guessed at that.But a size class doesn't tell us where the sheet might end up. It's up to the system whether to present the sheet on one side of the map. We wanted the content of the sheet, the map and navigation to all agree on the layout.Instead of guessing, we took a measurement of the sheet and the map. And we compared the horizontal position of the sheet with the bounds of the map.struct SheetPlacement: Equatable {
    var map: ClosedRange<CGFloat>?
    var sheet: ClosedRange<CGFloat>?

    var isDocked: Bool {
        guard let map, let sheet else { return false }
        let width = map.upperBound - map.lowerBound
        return sheet.lowerBound - map.lowerBound > width / 4
            && map.upperBound - sheet.upperBound < 32
    }
}
Each view reports its frame through SwiftUI’s onGeometryChange modifier. We compare the horizontal bounds of the sheet and map to determine whether the sheet is docked. So if you pull up or down on a bottom sheet, our decision about whether to dock it doesn't change.Put the back button where the driver can reach itThe thresholds support our sheet-and-map layout. They aren't part of a broader SwiftUI docking system. Most importantly, we now provided the same answer about the position of the sheet to each of the views involved.Most obviously, this broke Messages. In our unfolded state, we showed a rail on the trailing edge of the navigation bar. When the sheet docked, it covered this rail (including the back button).A screen can feel open, yet still be limiting for the user. We wanted the same map and navigation actions regardless of where the sheet appeared.When the sheet is docked, we hide the navigation bar. But we add those actions over the top-leading corner of the map. When the sheet returns to the bottom, we restore the normal navigation bar..toolbar(placement.isDocked ? .hidden : .automatic,
         for: .navigationBar)
.overlay(alignment: .topLeading) {
    if placement.isDocked {
        controls()
            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
            .padding(.trailing, placement.trailingInset)
    }
}
This inset on the trailing edge represents how much of the screen the docked sheet would occupy. That way, the driver could see some of the map and the controls.And we made the controls respond to Dynamic Type. ViewThatFits first shows them in a single row. But as the available space shrinks, it pushes the actions on the map into a second row.Even on a wide device, there may not be enough room for the controls once we add the sheet and larger text. So we included a medium height on our bottom sheet. That way the driver could see details of the sheet while still seeing some of the map.Give a side sheet useful statesIt didn't make sense to include this option when the sheet was docked. The map was already visible next to it. And at half height, the content of the sheet got cut off.So we dropped the medium detent when the sheet was docked. In the bottom sheet layout, we leave it up to the content.And we needed to add a transition. If the sheet was at medium height when it docked, taking away that option could leave it in a collapsed state. So in our sheet, we moved that selection to the expanded state. The driver shouldn't need to re-open the details if the size of the window changed.This was a subtle change to the state of our sheet, but one that affected its appearance. Now the options in our sheet depend on its placement. And when the sheet moves from one placement to another, we preserve useful actions.Frame the route in the space that remainsThe map required two changes. First, the size of the window wasn't the same as the area we could use for the route. The docked sheet covered the trailing side of the map.We add trailing safe-area padding to the map based on the docked sheet’s width. This keeps the route framed in the visible area while the map continues to draw behind the sheet.Second, when we unfold, fold, rotate or dock a sheet, the visible area changes. MapKit could keep its camera across these sizes. And the route could well fall out of view.We check the size of the map and its safe area. We cancel any pending refit of the map and wait 300ms for the layout to complete. Then we refit the route.refitTask?.cancel()
refitTask = Task {
    try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(300))
    guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }
    var transaction = Transaction()
    transaction.disablesAnimations = true
    withTransaction(transaction) { cameraPosition = .automatic }
    await Task.yield()
    withAnimation(.easeInOut(duration: 0.3)) {
        cameraPosition = .region(region)
    }
}
Why do we clear the camera? Well, if we applied the same region, we might do nothing. And 300ms was something we added, not required by the platform.Taken together, the refit and inset allow the route to reflect what the driver could see in the window. Just changing the size of the window wasn't sufficient.Keep the checks about using the appWith these changes we could check, for example, whether after unfolding the window, the driver could see the route, read its details, access the actions on the map and get back from Messages.We also need to check larger text and switch layouts while a sheet is already open. Launching directly into a wide window can miss problems with the camera position or sheet selection during a resize.In our Android Fold work, I found a bottom sheet in the middle of the screen could hide the route on a wide window. We changed the layout so the details would appear next to the map. That change required decisions about space. So did many of the ones I made in my app.In its October 28 announcement of the new MacBook Pro with a touchscreen, Apple suggested developers could try touch interactions in their iPadOS and iOS apps on this display. We haven't tested our app on this hardware. For an app that runs on Mac, we'd check the same sheet and navigation behavior with pointer, keyboard, and touch input.Then capture the layout for the App StoreOnce we'd fixed my app, we could have taken screenshots of its behavior on the unfolded screen. To do that, our capture tools needed workarounds.In our beta testing, XCUIScreen.main picked the cover display even when the inner panel was in use. Our fastlane helper selected the largest nonblank image from all the screens.let screenshot = XCUIScreen.screens
    .map { $0.screenshot() }
    .sorted { area($0.image) > area($1.image) }
    .first { !isBlank($0.image) }
    ?? XCUIScreen.main.screenshot()
In our setup, the inactive display produced a black image. For our fixture scenes, choosing the largest nonblank image let us select the active display’s screenshot. We also checked capture dimensions to reject images from the wrong display.After rebooting, the simulator lost its unfolded posture. To work around this, we wrote a watcher that restored the unfolded posture after reboot.We launched our app directly into fixture scenes because test taps could reach the cover display instead of the visible panel. This made captures repeatable, but didn't replace testing user navigation. But this didn't eliminate the value of testing navigation in the app for someone using it.In its screenshot specs (check as of October 28) Apple does allow 2853 x 2007 or 2007 x 2853 images of the unfolded screen. Our capture tool rotates and resizes the image for use in the landscape slot.Apple says from April 2027, apps using the iOS 27.1 SDK or later will need Duo screenshots.The screenshots showed the result of the app fixes: reachable controls, sheet states suited to their placement, and a route framed in the visible map area.Before you take screenshots of your own Duo, go into key screens for your app and move the window around them. What gets selected, what moves and what doesn't? These checks helped us find the fixes we needed before submitting our screenshots and getting our app up to date with a first class Duo experience.

Every few months, another high-profile document leak hits the headlines. A government agency releases redacted court transcripts, or a corporate legal team publishes "censored" financial filings. Within ten minutes, someone on Reddit or Hacker News drags their cursor across the black bars, hits , pastes it into Notepad, and suddenly, classified names and bank balances appear in plain text.The culprit is almost always the same: .Someone took a PDF editor, drew an opaque black annotation rectangle over the text, and exported it. Visually, the sensitive data was hidden. Structurally, the raw text glyphs remained untouched inside the PDF’s content streams.Even worse? When casual users try to fix this using popular "Free Online PDF Editors," they commit an even greater security cardinal sin: uploading unredacted, confidential documents to an unknown cloud server over the public internet.In this article, let’s tear down why PDF redaction fails at the binary level, and walk through how we built an irreversible, zero-server document sanitization engine running 100% client-side in the browser.1. The Anatomy of a PDF LeakA PDF is not an image; it is an object-oriented document format built on postscript-like streams.When you see a line of text on a page, the PDF structure looks something like this:ps
BT
/F1 12 Tf
72 712 Td
(CONFIDENTIAL: User SSN is 000-12-3456) Tj
ET
If an editor merely adds a black rectangle above that text block:ps
0 0 0 rg
70 710 200 15 re
f
The rendering engine executes sequentially: it paints the text, then draws a black box on top of it. The text stream remains intact. Anyone using a parser (or even a standard browser preview) can still extract the text, run search queries on it, or inspect the document tree.To truly redact a PDF, you have two choices:: Parse the content stream, locate character matrices intersecting the bounding box, slice the PostScript operators, and rewrite the font mapping tables. (Extremely fragile; edge cases with kerning, font sub-setting, and vector graphics frequently fail or leave phantom character artifacts).True Physical Raster Sanitization: Render the target page into an isolated graphics context, physically overwrite the pixel memory with opaque blocks, discard the underlying text objects entirely, and reconstruct the page.For security-critical applications, Option 2 is mathematically foolproof. If the glyphs no longer exist in the byte array, no parser can ever reconstruct them.2. The Cloud Privacy ParadoxBefore diving into code, let's address the elephant in the room: cloud-based PDF utilities.When an employee needs to hide trade secrets or medical records, they search for . They upload their sensitive file to a SaaS platform.The user’s goal is .The SaaS model requires uploading  over HTTPS to a remote container, storing it in an ephemeral S3 bucket, and processing it on a remote VM.Even with TLS and "we delete files after 1 hour" promises, you are expanding your attack surface by transmitting raw PII outside your local machine.The web has evolved. With modern HTML5 Canvas, WebAssembly, and typed arrays, the browser sandbox is more than capable of handling enterprise-grade PDF manipulation with zero bytes leaving the client.3. Engineering the Engine: Hybrid Sanitization PipelineWhen building our client-side redaction tool, we faced an architectural dilemma:If you rasterize an entire 200-page document just to redact two words on page 3, you destroy vector crispness for 198 pristine pages and blow up the file size from 2MB to 80MB.Our solution was a Selective Hybrid Pipeline:: Cloned losslessly as raw vector streams.: Irrevocably burned in-memory via high-DPI canvas buffers.Here is the architectural workflow:4. The Code: Irreversible In-Browser DestructionLet’s look at the core execution pipeline implemented with  and .Step 1: Capturing Normalized CoordinatesThe user draws redaction boxes over a responsive preview container. We record coordinates as normalized percentages (0.0 to 1.0) rather than raw screen pixels, ensuring accuracy across any zoom factor or device pixel ratio:typescript
export interface RedactionBox {
  id: string;
  pageIndex: number; // 0-based
  x: number;         // 0.0 - 1.0
  y: number;         // 0.0 - 1.0
  width: number;     // 0.0 - 1.0
  height: number;    // 0.0 - 1.0
  color: 'black' | 'white';
}
When the user clicks "Export Sanitized Document", we read the binary buffer locally:typescript
import { PDFDocument } from 'pdf-lib';
async function sanitizePdf(file: File, redactions: RedactionBox[], pdfJsDoc: any): Promise<Blob> {
  const arrayBuffer = await file.arrayBuffer();
  // 1. Load original document strictly in-memory
  const originalPdf = await PDFDocument.load(arrayBuffer, { ignoreEncryption: true });
  const newPdf = await PDFDocument.create();
  const totalPages = originalPdf.getPageCount();
  for (let i = 0; i < totalPages; i++) {
    const pageBoxes = redactions.filter((b) => b.pageIndex === i);
    if (pageBoxes.length === 0) {
      // BRANCH A: Clean page -> Lossless vector clone
      const [copiedPage] = await newPdf.copyPages(originalPdf, [i]);
      newPdf.addPage(copiedPage);
    } else {
      // BRANCH B: Dirty page -> Physical pixel wipeout
      const pdfPage = await pdfJsDoc.getPage(i + 1);

      // 2.0x scale guarantees ~200 DPI crisp print quality
      const scale = 2.0;
      const viewport = pdfPage.getViewport({ scale });
      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
      canvas.width = Math.floor(viewport.width);
      canvas.height = Math.floor(viewport.height);
      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d')!;
      // Render base document into canvas
      ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
      await pdfPage.render({ canvasContext: ctx, viewport }).promise;
      // Physically burn opaque pixels directly into the canvas buffer!
      pageBoxes.forEach((box) => {
        const rx = Math.round(box.x * viewport.width);
        const ry = Math.round(box.y * viewport.height);
        const rw = Math.round(box.width * viewport.width);
        const rh = Math.round(box.height * viewport.height);
        ctx.fillStyle = box.color === 'white' ? '#ffffff' : '#000000';
        ctx.fillRect(rx, ry, rw, rh);
      });
      // Convert sanitized canvas back to binary image stream
      const sanitizedBlob = await new Promise<Blob | null>((res) =>
        canvas.toBlob(res, 'image/jpeg', 0.94)
      );
      if (!sanitizedBlob) throw new Error('Rasterization failure');
      const imgBytes = await sanitizedBlob.arrayBuffer();
      const embeddedImg = await newPdf.embedJpg(imgBytes);
      // Re-create the page preserving original physical dimensions
      const originalPage = originalPdf.getPage(i);
      const { width, height } = originalPage.getSize();
      const newPage = newPdf.addPage([width, height]);
      newPage.drawImage(embeddedImg, {
        x: 0,
        y: 0,
        width,
        height,
      });
    }
  }
  // 3. Serialize and return local blob URL
  const finalPdfBytes = await newPdf.save();
  return new Blob([finalPdfBytes], { type: 'application/pdf' });
}
Executing high-resolution rasterization in a browser tab comes with pitfalls:Garbage Collection Pressure:Large PDFs can generate hundreds of megabytes of raw image data during processing.Never retain canvas instances in global variables.Set canvas.width = 0; canvas.height = 0; after converting to blob to free memory immediately.Always call URL.revokeObjectURL(prevUrl) when regenerating downloads.Touch & Pan Frictionless UX:Users redact on laptops, iPads, and trackpads. Implementing an overlay that distinguishes between  and  requires a drag threshold:typescript
const DRAG_THRESHOLD = 8; // Screen pixels
if (screenDistance < DRAG_THRESHOLD) {
  // Treat as tap or gesture, do not draw accidental mini-boxes
  return;
}
6. The Verdict: Local-First is the Real Future of Utility SoftwareFor years, developers were trained to default to the cloud:"Need to convert a PDF? Spin up an AWS Lambda with Poppler.""Need to redact an invoice? Send it to our REST endpoint."This architecture was never about user convenience; it was driven by legacy browser limitations and SaaS monetization models.Today, with the power of modern client-side engines:: Deployable for $0 on Cloudflare Pages or GitHub Pages.True zero-knowledge privacy: Your sensitive tax files or medical records never leave your RAM.Zero data liability for builders: You cannot leak user data if you never store it in the first place.If you are manipulating user files in 2026, ask yourself: Does this data really need to touch your cloud backend? Chances are, the user's browser is already more than fast enough to do it itself.

A distributed lock is not a bigger mutexA distributed lock is not a bigger mutex. It is a different animal wearing the same costume, and the costume is what gets people hurt.Distributed locks look deceptively similar to local mutexes. You acquire, you perform a critical operation, you release. That resemblance is dangerous.A local mutex lives inside one failure domain. A distributed lock spans processes and networks and clocks and storage, along with failure detectors that can disagree with each other. The lock service may correctly decide that ownership changed while the previous holder is still running or paused or partitioned, or while it is quietly carrying delayed requests.The central question is whether an obsolete client can still produce effects after its authority has ended. Martin Kleppmann frames the same divide as locks used for efficiency versus locks used for correctness. In the correctness case duplicate execution becomes corruption rather than wasted work.A lock is not a critical sectionConsider a lease-backed lock around a reconciliation job.Lease lease = lockService.acquire("order:" + orderId, Duration.ofSeconds(15));

try {
    reconcile(orderId);
} finally {
    lockService.release(lease);
}
The code is locally reasonable and globally unsafe. Suppose the process pauses for 30 seconds after acquisition. Maybe garbage collection stalls it, maybe the CPU is starved, maybe it is paging, maybe something sends it a stop signal. The lease expires, another worker acquires the lock, and the original process later resumes. Nothing in the resumed process proves that authority still exists.A delayed network request creates the same failure even when the process never pauses at all. Checking the lease immediately before a write does not close the race, because execution can pause in the gap between the check and the write. Kleppmann uses this stale holder problem to show why even a perfect lock service does not automatically make protected resource access safe.Coordination systems can provide strong ownership semantics without controlling the resource that actually matters. The ZooKeeper lock recipe uses ephemeral sequential znodes and gives the lock to the client with the lowest sequence number. etcd ties lock ownership to a lease and releases the lock when that lease expires. Those mechanisms establish authority inside the coordinator. They do nothing to stop an old process from writing to an external resource after ownership has changed.Leases trade deadlock for stale ownershipLeases exist because permanent locks have a severe liveness problem. A crashed owner can block progress forever. Time-bounded authority lets another participant proceed after expiration, but now time becomes part of the safety argument.Redis Redlock computes a remaining validity window from the elapsed acquisition time and assumes local clocks progress at roughly the same rate with bounded drift relative to the TTL. Current Redis documentation also warns that TTL expiration does not use a monotonic clock, and that a shift in the wall clock can compromise consistency.Lease expiration is not necessarily a single instant that everyone observes at once. Consul documents TTL as a lower bound for invalidation. Expiration will not occur before the TTL, but it may occur later. Consul warns about clock skew too. More fundamentally, it notes that failure detection can produce false positives and release a lock while the owner is still alive. Disabling those checks improves safety at the expense of liveness. Faster failure detection improves progress but raises the risk of overlapping authority.Lease renewal reduces probability, not category. Extending a TTL helps a long-running operation, but it cannot make networks timely, or process pauses bounded. Redis documentation now states that lock retention must not be assumed merely because the owning process is still alive, and it recommends fencing tokens for work that runs long.Fencing moves safety to the resourceA fencing token is a monotonically increasing value issued whenever ownership changes. Every protected operation carries that token. The resource remembers the highest token it has accepted and rejects any request carrying an older value. This converts a timing problem into an ordering problem. A stale process may resume, and an old RPC may arrive late, but neither one can overwrite state once a newer owner has advanced the fence.The essential logic belongs at the write boundary, not only in the lock client.long token = lockService.acquire("account:" + accountId).fencingToken();

int updated = jdbc.update("""
    UPDATE accounts
    SET state = ?, fence_token = ?
    WHERE id = ? AND fence_token <= ?
    """, state, token, accountId, token);

if (updated != 1) throw new StaleLockHolderException();
The database performs the comparison and the state change atomically. A request carrying token 41 cannot modify a row after token 42 has been accepted. The token has to be ordered and durable and scoped to the protected resource, and it must not be casually reset when the coordinator restarts. A random lock identifier is useful for safe release but it is not a fence, because randomness establishes identity rather than order. Redis uses random values to avoid deleting another client's lock, and separately recommends fencing for consistency.Several coordinators expose building blocks for this pattern. Consul increments  on acquisition and documents the triple of (Key, LockIndex, Session) as a sequencer that can identify a stale request. ZooKeeper sequential nodes provide monotonically increasing sequence suffixes. etcd exposes creation revisions for election leadership and supports transactionally testing that the leadership revision still matches. The important property is not which coordinator you picked. It is whether the downstream resource actually enforces the ordering information.Failure modes survive correct acquisitionCorrect acquisition can still coexist with unsafe effects. A partition can prevent renewal while leaving access to the protected database intact. A lock server can fail over while an application thread stays paused. A retry can duplicate a side effect that was never idempotent.A fencing token can be generated correctly and then ignored by one write path. It can be compared without atomicity, or stored only in memory, or reused after a restore, or scoped to the wrong thing. These are protocol failures rather than lock library bugs. Consul makes the advisory nature explicit. Its lock does not itself stop clients from reading or writing the underlying key without ownership.Some effects cannot naturally enforce fencing. Email delivery, a third-party API, or a legacy device may not expose a conditional operation that rejects a token lower than X. Correctness then has to move to a boundary that can reject duplicates or conflicting state. Idempotency keys can make a retried API request safe when the service supports them, and a database uniqueness constraint can reject a duplicate state transition represented by the same key. A distributed lock cannot retroactively make an unfenceable side effect exclusive.Testing has to attack stale authority rather than only lock contention. Useful fault injection pauses a holder beyond its TTL. It partitions the holder from the coordinator but not from storage. It delays writes until after reassignment, restarts the coordinator, forces renewal to fail, and reorders retries. The acceptance criterion is stronger than observing that only one lock owner was recorded. The protected resource must reject effects from every superseded epoch. These scenarios follow directly from the documented risks around lease expiration, false failure detection, and delayed execution.The lock is only half the protocolDistributed locks are safest when you treat them as advisory coordination plus an explicit mechanism for effect safety. Leases restore liveness by letting ownership expire, but expiration is exactly what creates stale holders. Consensus-backed coordinators improve the authority decision, yet they cannot erase a paused process or a delayed packet. Fencing tokens close that gap only when the protected resource validates them atomically and durably. Where fencing is impossible, something else has to carry the correctness burden, whether that is idempotency, a transaction, or a redesign. The dangerous distributed lock is not the one that occasionally fails to acquire. It is the one that looks successful while obsolete authority can still mutate reality.
